WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.199
Hello and welcome to mCoding! 
This is James Murphy.

00:00:03.199 --> 00:00:07.131
I am doing another giveaway. So, if you'd like
a professional license to PyCharm,

00:00:07.131 --> 00:00:11.096
which is the editor that I'm currently using, then
check the description.

00:00:11.096 --> 00:00:17.219
In this video, we're going over whether or not a bunch of 
built-in things in Python make copies of your data.

00:00:17.219 --> 00:00:19.935
This is actually inspired by one 
of my previous videos.

00:00:19.935 --> 00:00:23.647
I had a lot of commenters, rightfully worried
that looping over a list this way

00:00:23.647 --> 00:00:27.141
in order to loop over it backwards 
might be making a copy.

00:00:27.141 --> 00:00:30.915
I got suggestions to use 
a slice of a list instead.

00:00:30.915 --> 00:00:34.830
And, in the wild, I've even seen people fall 
back to using error-prone indices

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:36.671
just to avoid this issue.

00:00:36.671 --> 00:00:40.915
Well, it's definitely clear this index-based
approach doesn't make a copy of my list.

00:00:40.915 --> 00:00:43.013
But it's so much less readable.

00:00:43.013 --> 00:00:46.905
So, I'm happy to tell you that reversed() does
not make a copy of your sequence.

00:00:46.905 --> 00:00:50.585
If you want to loop over 
something backwards, this is the way to do it.

00:00:50.585 --> 00:00:53.488
This isn't a foolproof method to 
tell whether or not a copy is made.

00:00:53.488 --> 00:00:56.320
But we can actually get 
an inclination that one is not made

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:59.066
by printing out 
the reversed of the list.

00:00:59.066 --> 00:01:01.529
Notice that instead of getting 
an actual list back,

00:01:01.529 --> 00:01:04.929
we get this list reverse 
iterator object.

00:01:04.929 --> 00:01:09.349
If you see something that says 
like an iterator or a view or a generator,

00:01:09.349 --> 00:01:12.682
that should be a clue that a copy 
probably isn't being made.

00:01:12.682 --> 00:01:16.408
Just for comparison, notice that if 
we use this fancy slice of a list,

00:01:16.408 --> 00:01:18.555
then we actually get 
another list back.

00:01:18.555 --> 00:01:22.756
So although it's shorter in this case, 
it is also making a copy.

00:01:22.756 --> 00:01:27.513
So then how is reversed() able to loop over
the list without making a copy?

00:01:27.513 --> 00:01:32.061
Well, here's an implementation of a class
that acts the way that reversed does.

00:01:32.061 --> 00:01:36.757
The key idea in all of these ways of iterating
is to define your own iterator.

00:01:36.757 --> 00:01:41.064
An iterator is just an object that you can
repeatedly ask for elements.

00:01:41.064 --> 00:01:45.391
The way that you ask it for an element is
by calling its __next__ method.

00:01:45.391 --> 00:01:48.870
This is also what's called by 
the built-in next function.

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:53.370
If there are any more elements left to iterate
over, then next returns the next one.

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:58.285
If there aren't any more elements to iterate
over, you signal that by raising a stop iteration.

00:01:58.285 --> 00:02:03.763
Technically speaking, an iterator must also
have an __iter__ method that returns self.

00:02:03.763 --> 00:02:10.382
This is a little bit confusing because the magic 
__iter__ method is used to define an iterable, not an iterator.

00:02:10.382 --> 00:02:14.212
Iterables are just things that you can ask
for an iterator from.

00:02:14.212 --> 00:02:19.358
Whereas, an iterator you can think of
 more as an in-flight stream of elements.

00:02:19.358 --> 00:02:21.346
So all the return self really 
says is that

00:02:21.346 --> 00:02:26.588
if you want to get a stream of elements 
from a stream of elements, just return the stream.

00:02:26.588 --> 00:02:30.030
Most of the important logic 
is in the next method.

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:34.152
So all we do when we construct the reversed
object is store reference to the sequence.

00:02:34.152 --> 00:02:37.469
Initialize an index to the last 
element of the sequence.

00:02:37.469 --> 00:02:39.046
And do some error checking.

00:02:39.046 --> 00:02:41.468
Every time you ask a reversed 
for the next element,

00:02:41.468 --> 00:02:44.000
it gives you the element 
at its current index.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.082
And then subtracts one 
from the index.

00:02:46.082 --> 00:02:49.283
The last or I guess first valid index is 0.

00:02:49.283 --> 00:02:52.194
So, after returning that one, 
our index gets set to -1.

00:02:52.194 --> 00:02:56.096
So, if we see minus one, then 
we know there are no more elements.

00:02:56.096 --> 00:03:00.720
So as you can see this allows us to iterate
over the entire sequence without making a copy of it.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.032
The built-in reverse is actually going to
do something more like this

00:03:03.033 --> 00:03:06.042
that actually does things in terms 
of this next method.

00:03:06.042 --> 00:03:10.714
A common way to do something similar without
as much code is to use a generator instead.

00:03:10.714 --> 00:03:12.821
This generator code 
does essentially the same thing.

00:03:12.821 --> 00:03:15.504
And is quite a bit clearer 
in what it's doing.

00:03:15.504 --> 00:03:18.402
But the major difference here is 
that the error checking doesn't happen

00:03:18.402 --> 00:03:20.054
until you actually start the generator

00:03:20.054 --> 00:03:23.297
which happens when you 
actually ask for the first element.

00:03:23.297 --> 00:03:26.634
Whereas, the class solution did 
the error checking in its init function.

00:03:26.634 --> 00:03:30.664
Of course, doing error checking earlier rather
than later is usually a good idea.

00:03:30.664 --> 00:03:32.257
But this is an option.

00:03:32.257 --> 00:03:33.112
So then the question is:

00:03:33.112 --> 00:03:38.501
Which of the major built-ins actually 
make copies and which don't?

00:03:38.501 --> 00:03:43.659
Aside from sorted, most of the built-in
 that make copies are containers themselves.

00:03:43.659 --> 00:03:44.785
And that kind of makes sense.

00:03:44.785 --> 00:03:49.399
If you ask to make a new container using an 
old one, then you're kind of asking to make a copy.

00:03:49.399 --> 00:03:53.119
On the other hand, there are a 
bunch of them that don't make copies.

00:03:53.119 --> 00:03:56.241
So enumerate, filter, inter, map, 
reversed, and zip,

00:03:56.241 --> 00:04:01.317
any of the dictionary views and 
generator comprehensions don't make copies.

00:04:01.317 --> 00:04:04.271
Once again, you can mostly 
tell by printing them out.

00:04:04.271 --> 00:04:09.285
The ones that make copies look like 
they've returned a new version of the original thing.

00:04:09.285 --> 00:04:15.345
Whereas, the ones that don't make copies 
are returning these special purpose looking objects.

00:04:15.345 --> 00:04:19.771
As is often the case in Python though,
 there are almost always exceptions.

00:04:19.771 --> 00:04:23.364
You're able to change how a lot 
of built-ins interact with your classes.

00:04:23.364 --> 00:04:25.614
For instance, slicing a list 
makes a copy.

00:04:25.614 --> 00:04:28.561
Because that's the behavior 
that the built-in list shows.

00:04:28.561 --> 00:04:31.399
But you can define your own 
get item method for your classes.

00:04:31.399 --> 00:04:33.356
And do whatever you 
want with it.

00:04:33.356 --> 00:04:36.175
And that's exactly what NumPy arrays do.

00:04:36.175 --> 00:04:38.929
Things are not looking good 
if you just print it out.

00:04:38.929 --> 00:04:41.226
Both of these objects 
are numpy arrays.

00:04:41.226 --> 00:04:43.909
So, you might think that 
reversed is a copy.

00:04:43.909 --> 00:04:48.254
But NumPy arrays are actually 
just very thin views around raw memory.

00:04:48.254 --> 00:04:50.804
And printing out the data attribute 
of both of these arrays,

00:04:50.804 --> 00:04:54.184
we see that they're sharing the 
same exact memory object.

00:04:54.184 --> 00:04:57.855
Contrary to the way that lists behave, 
no copy was made here.

00:04:57.855 --> 00:05:01.200
The way that NumPy achieves 
this is by using strides.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:05.871
Strides tell you how many bytes you need to jump if 
you want to get to the next element of the array.

00:05:05.871 --> 00:05:09.648
These are tuples instead of just numbers because 
arrays can be multi-dimensional.

00:05:09.648 --> 00:05:12.626
But for a 1D array, we basically 
just have a number.

00:05:12.626 --> 00:05:17.362
The `4` here tells me that from some element 
I need to jump four bytes to get to the next one.

00:05:17.362 --> 00:05:22.218
That's because NumPy chose a 32-bit 
or 4 byte integer type for this array.

00:05:22.218 --> 00:05:24.047
So, all I need to do to get 
a reversed array

00:05:24.047 --> 00:05:28.807
is start at the end and jump by negative
e 4 for each element instead of positive 4.

00:05:28.807 --> 00:05:32.876
It's a very simple, clever and effective
 way to do things efficiently.

00:05:32.876 --> 00:05:35.627
I wouldn't expect anything less from NumPy.

00:05:35.627 --> 00:05:40.125
And finally, we spend all this time talking
 about how to avoid making copies of things.

00:05:40.125 --> 00:05:42.394
But I have to say that copies 
are not always bad.

00:05:42.394 --> 00:05:45.122
Sometimes you really need 
a copy.

00:05:45.122 --> 00:05:47.357
The canonical example of when 
you'd want to copy is

00:05:47.357 --> 00:05:51.189
if you're iterating over something and
 mutating it at the same time.

00:05:51.189 --> 00:05:55.008
For instance, here I have a, b, c 
mapped to 1, 2, 3.

00:05:55.008 --> 00:06:00.531
I want to go into the dictionary and add
 uppercase A, B and C with the same values 1, 2 and 3.

00:06:00.531 --> 00:06:04.047
Seems simple enough. 
Iterate over key value pairs.

00:06:04.047 --> 00:06:08.337
Convert the key to uppercase and 
assign it the same value.

00:06:08.337 --> 00:06:11.822
It seems straightforward but we got
 slapped with this runtime error.

00:06:11.822 --> 00:06:15.027
Dictionary changes size during iteration.

00:06:15.027 --> 00:06:18.345
If you're not familiar how dictionaries
 are implemented in terms of hash tables,

00:06:18.345 --> 00:06:21.014
this might be a very confusing error.

00:06:21.014 --> 00:06:24.433
Why does Python care if I modify
 something while I'm looping over it?

00:06:24.433 --> 00:06:27.498
The very short and simple explanation 
is that modifying the dictionary

00:06:27.498 --> 00:06:31.112
might cause the dictionary to have to
 move all the elements around.

00:06:31.112 --> 00:06:35.030
If that were to happen then the iterator
 might basically just lose its place.

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:37.728
The solution is to manually make the copy.

00:06:37.728 --> 00:06:40.393
Making a copy here means that 
we exhaust the iterator

00:06:40.393 --> 00:06:42.899
and collect all the results 
into the list.

00:06:42.899 --> 00:06:44.382
So the iterator is completely done

00:06:44.382 --> 00:06:46.929
before we ever start to modify 
the dictionary.

00:06:46.929 --> 00:06:49.465
So, there you have it.
 I hope this helped a bit.

